hi there Steve Kaufman here today I want  to talk about language learning goals  and frustrations but before I get into  the subject I want to thank everyone  that sent me those nice birthday  greetings it's very nice to hear from  all of you it's nice to feel that  somehow I'm connected to a great  community of people who share a common  interest with me which is language  learning I haven't been able to reply to  all of you but I do appreciate it very  much it's also encouraging to find out  that quite a few people actually listen  through to the middle of my video where  I mentioned the fact that it was my  birthday for those of you who are  curious I was I am now 68 no I have  lunch earlier this week with a college  professor who's head of a a language  department in a university and he told  me that a majority of students who  enroll in Asian languages quit after the  first year which I was quite surprised  to hear but then I did some research on  the internet and in fact I wrote a blog  post about this at my blog you'll find  the link to my blog in the description  of this video and discovered that you  know the attrition rate the dropout rate  on all kinds of language programs is  very very high this is true if it's  whether it's online learning whether  it's they had an example in Australia  where 94% of kids taking Mandarin  Chinese in the school system didn't  complete the program and in the end it  ended up being Mandarin speakers  teaching Mandarin speakers Mandarin  because in effect the only kids that  stayed in the program were Chinese  immigrants who already spoke Mandarin I  guess they needed an easy credit we have  the same situation here so but getting  back to this professor in our discussion  he I said but why did they quit and he  said well they discover how hard it is  and it's a lot of work so they don't  want to do it and  during our conversation we also  expressed some dissatisfaction with the  recent trends in language acquisition  that's that are moving away from grammar  and it's more as he put a touchy-feely  communication and stuff like that well  you know I there are lots of fads in  language instruction I'm not a great fan  of the role-playing they have a thing in  Canada called the task-based language  learning where they sort of create these  false scenarios where you pretend you  are you know the checkout person in a  store or whatever it might be and people  play roles I don't believe in that as  you know I believe in a lot of listening  and reading as much as possible to  subjects of interest this becomes easier  as you progress in the language and at  some point you started speaking and  hopefully you speak in situations that  are meaningful to you and not artificial  situations that to me is an ideal  scenario but what was interesting to me  was this professor with this enormous  dropout rate was mostly upset about the  fact that students weren't prepared to  put in the hard work that language  instruction was methodology or pedagogy  was moving away from the center  traditional emphasis on grammar and I  thought myself you know I wonder whether  at that University trying to figure out  how to reduce the attrition rate the  dropout rate whether that in fact would  not be a worthwhile subject of inquiry  and so then I thought to myself you know  and I should add that one of the  comments I think on Facebook where my  blog article about this dropout rate  appeared was a student who said that he  was studying Russian and the teacher  came in on day one and said class you're  gonna be doing a lot of writing a lot of  memorizing and that's the only way  you're gonna learn and he found this  message quite discouraging and so I  thought to myself Oh  mm-hmm how do we encourage people to  stay with the program but beyond that  what is the goal what is the goal of  someone taking  you know an Asian language Russian  Spanish whatever it might be in a  university a school it's over what are  they trying to achieve the assumption  with this sort of role-playing is that  they want to be a you know checkout girl  in a supermarket or they want to develop  some very you know specific skills for  specific functions and I've always felt  that that was not really that useful  because even if you have a script for a  specific function you can't control  what's gonna come at you and so that  actually you need a much larger  knowledge base in order to be able to  cope with these apparently simple tasks  so what are they what are the possible  goals in a university program well I  mean universities offer programs and  things like music appreciation Gender  Studies you know art the history of art  I mean these programs are not  necessarily aimed exclusively at the  person who's going to become a curator  at a at an art museum or you know music  music critic for a newspaper it's just  general education now I have often been  critical of the way we fund our  universities because a lot of this  general education if the person is  genuinely interested that person can  acquire much of that invention education  on their own as is of course the case  also with language instruction but get  me back the idea of goals if the goal is  say in a Russian program is to to make  the person stay with it enjoy it get a  sense of the language get a sense of the  culture be able to access some of the  works of the culture I think all of  those things would be very meaningful to  try and enforce the absolute correct  production of all the cases and the  aspects of verbs in Russia upon someone  who has had essentially no I mean going  into had no exposure to Russia seems to  me  guaranteed to discourage  majority of students a small minority  who are very motivated will pursue it  and will also go out and get you know  the other input that they that they need  but it just struck me that the goal of  the university should be how do we  retain these people how do we make it  interesting for them how do we provide  them with something that they can take  away from this year that's gonna be or  eventually hopefully for year for years  that's going to be beneficial to them  those that you know after two or three  years really want to hone their Russian  skills at that time it's not too late to  go in there and for those that really  want to be you know translators or  interpreter it's fine do something for  them but the emphasis on predict correct  production of the language and and  forcing on them a lot of rote  memorization and writing exercises and  so forth when the majority of them are  not going to be using Russian in any  case and with the result that the  majority quit you know that strikes me  as not a very good approach furthermore  you know a university program like here  in Canada students pay around three to  five thousand dollars depending on which  program which province they're in and  whereas the the cost of the university  education is probably around thirty  thousand dollars I mean the universities  charged foreign students twenty six  thousand dollars and claim that they  cover their costs I don't believe them  so I'm assuming that it's at least  twenty-six thousand dollars it anyway  it's not worth quibbling typically  students have five courses in a year  so therefore if one of them is a  language course that course costs  someone the taxpayer or someone four or  five thousand dollars and if the  majority of students quit then for the  majority of students that four or five  thousand dollars is simply thrown away  now since the taxpayer picks up 80  percent of the bill  I don't think students should be allowed  to take these language courses unless  they have demonstrated that they have  the willpower the commitment the  interest to actually stay with it and  the best way to do that is to  give them some kind of self learning  system likely not just link it could be  anything rosetta stone doesn't matter I  see I'll demonstrate first that you are  interested enough in this language that  you are gonna stick with it and once  you've demonstrated that then you are to  apply for the language program now of  course if the students are paying their  own shot then that's fine they can pay  for but if the taxpayers paying for it  there should be some requirement and one  of the we once dealt with a company in  France a large company that offered  language training to their employees but  only to those that had for six months  stayed with some kind of a distance or  online or self learning program to  demonstrate their interest and I think  this only makes sense but again it comes  back to the goals what are we trying to  achieve and I think you know in my own  case I mean Russian check the languages  that I've been learning Korean dabbling  in Korea and stuff like that I'm doing  it for my own enjoyment to access the  the culture to broaden my horizons my  perspectives events to go to the country  and communicate I don't have to nail  down every case Andy in order to derive  significant benefit and maybe students  should be asked what is your goal  why are you studying these languages  because you're interested in the culture  in which case we don't need to be so  heavy-handed on enforcing you know  absolutely correct production of the  language especially if it turns out that  that discourages learners so you know I  think wherever languages are taught  given the high dropout rate frustration  rate attrition rate the number one  consideration should be how do we keep  people motivated how do we keep people  with the program because if people put  in the time and if they are motivated  they will learn they will learn that may  not become perfect but under the  existing system most of drop up so it  seems to me  time for another reproach dare I suggest  link thank you for listening bye for now
 

